.TH smi-project-prepare "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Project Command"
.SH NAME
smi-project-prepare \- start a new private project from a public template repository
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-project-prepare
\fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR \fITEMPLATE_REPO_URL\fR \fIPRIVATE_REPOS_URL\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBsmi-project-prepare\fR command makes a new private, closed source project from a public
template repository, and protects the template from an accidental push of the private code.
.PP
When run, it performs the following:
.IP \[bu] 2
Creates the bare private repository \fIPRIVATE_REPOS_URL\fR/\fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR.git with the
initial branch \fBmaster\fR, over ssh to the host of the URL.
.IP \[bu] 2
Clones the \fBmaster\fR branch of \fITEMPLATE_REPO_URL\fR into ./\fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR of the current
directory, with the template as the remote \fBupstream\fR.
.IP \[bu] 2
Adds the private repository as the remote \fBorigin\fR, and makes it the default push target with
.B remote.pushDefault
and
.BR "push.default simple" .
.IP \[bu] 2
Sets the push URL of \fBupstream\fR to \fBDISABLED\fR, so a push to the template fails. Fetching from
the template still works, so its later changes can be merged into the new project.
.IP \[bu] 2
Pushes \fBmaster\fR to \fBorigin\fR and sets it as the upstream branch, then prints the remotes, the
branches and the local Git configuration.
.PP
The command fails if an argument is missing, if \fIPRIVATE_REPOS_URL\fR is not an ssh URL with a host
and a folder, if the project name is empty, has a slash or starts with a dot, or if
\&./\fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR already exists.
.PP
The template code is left as it is. Cleaning up and renaming the copy is the next step, done by
\fBsmi-project-cleanup\fR(1) in ./\fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR
Name of the new project: the local folder, and the private repository \fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR.git.
.TP
\fITEMPLATE_REPO_URL\fR
Git URL of the template repository the new project starts from.
.TP
\fIPRIVATE_REPOS_URL\fR
ssh URL of the folder of the private repositories, without the repository name:
[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR:\fIpath\fR or ssh://[\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]/\fIpath\fR.
The command adds /\fINEW_PROJECT_NAME\fR.git to it, a trailing slash is dropped.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Make ./myproject from the setmy.info template. The private repositories are in
/srv/git/projects of git.example.com, reached as the user git:
.PP
.nf
.B smi-project-prepare myproject git@github.com:setmy-info/setmy.info.git \e
.B "    git@git.example.com:/srv/git/projects"
.fi
.PP
It runs \fBgit init \-\-bare /srv/git/projects/myproject.git\fR over ssh on git.example.com,
clones the template into ./myproject, and sets the remotes:
.PP
.nf
origin    git@git.example.com:/srv/git/projects/myproject.git (fetch, push)
upstream  git@github.com:setmy-info/setmy.info.git (fetch)
upstream  DISABLED (push)
.fi
.PP
The same with an ssh port of its own:
.PP
.nf
.B smi-project-prepare myproject git@github.com:setmy-info/setmy.info.git \e
.B "    ssh://git@git.example.com:2222/srv/git/projects"
.fi
